**F0 dual-harness result (2026-08-22): representation premise REFUTED.** Draft PR #878, workflow run `32561761076`, generic ORION + native ORION-Q + byte-identical replay all GREEN. Terminal: `QG7F_TWO_COORD_REDUCTION_REFUTED__TAG3_MULTI_COMM_S2_CONFIGURATION`.

The registered issue text contains a load-bearing premise that is not earned by QG-7c: it says QG-7c proves global Tag support <=2 in the comm-s2 sector. QG-7c T2 actually gives `wt(S) <= 3 + #comm-s2`; the weight-3 consolidation result is comm-s2-free/conditional on the still-open comm-s2 sector.

Frozen explicit counterexample to the narrower representation premise, local codes `I=0,X=1,Y=2,Z=3`:
- `S=(X,X,X)`, wt=3;
- anchored A `(XII,YII)`;
- comm-s2 B `(YYI,ZII)`, support `{0,1}`;
- comm-s2 C `(IYY,IZI)`, support `{1,2}`.

Both C blocks independently satisfy the exact M1 comm-s2 conditions, have no class-(0,0) Lemma-E single-zeroing coordinate, all three blocks anticommute internally and share labels `(0,1)`, yet B/C use different support pairs. Thus the next chain representation may **not** assume one common two-coordinate Tag support.

Authority remains deliberately narrow: `CHAIN_REPRESENTATION_COMPLETE=false`, `CHAIN_ALL_N=false`, `GLOBAL_BDOUBLEPRIME_COMPLETENESS=false`, `FIFTH_REGIME_FOUND=false`. The successor must either (a) enumerate overlapping comm-s2 support-pair hypergraphs inside higher-weight shared Tags, or (b) first prove a new global Tag-support reduction specifically for the actual remaining chain sector.
